\title{Correct rounding reduces to statements about ranges of bit patterns}
\taxon{Change}
\date{2026-11-23}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{changelog}
\tag{exhaustive}
\meta{pr}{1}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\code{\verb~|LibcMath/Exhaustive/~} gains the reduction from finite checks to \code{\verb~|Func.CorrectlyRounded~}, stated over hypotheses: a sound interpreter (\code{\verb~|IR.Func.IsComputedBy~}), an \code{\verb~|Oracle~}, and Boolean equations of the form \code{\verb~|checkRange O run mode first count = true~}. \pr{1}.}
\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|HoldsOnBits P lo hi~} says that \code{\verb~|P~} holds on the bit patterns from \code{\verb~|lo~} up to but excluding \code{\verb~|hi~}. Certificates for adjacent ranges join with \code{\verb~|HoldsOnBits.append~}, with no side condition about coverage to evaluate, and the range from \code{\verb~|0~} to \code{\verb~|2 ^ fmt.bitWidth~} gives \code{\verb~|P~} for every value of the format.}
\li{\code{\verb~|IR.Func.CorrectAt~} is correct rounding at one input and one rounding direction; \code{\verb~|IR.Func.correctlyRounded_of_holdsOnBits~} assembles the four directions.}
\li{\code{\verb~|Spec.exp_eval_towardZero~}: \code{\verb~|exp~} prescribes the same results toward zero and downward, so an oracle has to run for three of the four directions only.}
\li{\code{\verb~|LibcMath/SpecLemmas.lean~}: \code{\verb~|SameValue~} is a decidable equivalence and a runtime value encodes at most one result up to it.}
}
\p{What was left open is listed under [open problems](open-problems): [prob-7F7Z](prob-7F7Z), [prob-E4RT](prob-E4RT).}
